1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. c3 c5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Ne2 Qb6 8. Nf3
cxd4 9. cxd4 f6 10. exf6 Nxf6 11. O-O Bd6 12. Bf4 ({Various plans have been
tried in this book position.} 12. Nf4 {, once fashionable, has been discarded
owing to} O-O 13. Re1 Bd7 14. Nxe6 Rfe8 15. Bf5 Bb4 $1 {which favours Black.})
(12. Bd2 {and 12.Bc3 holds the black squares, but makes the bishop passive.}) (
{Simple development by} 12. Nc3 {and 13.Be3 is White's best try for an
advantage.}) 12... Qc7 ({Not so good as} 12... Bxf4 13. Nxf4 Qxb2 14. Re1 O-O
15. Nxe6 Bxe6 16. Rxe6 Ng4 $1 {with equality. [16...N-N5 is given, so it is
possible that 16...Nb4 was intended. JS]}) 13. Qd2 O-O 14. Bxd6 Qxd6 15. Qg5 $1
({Better than} 15. Qe3 Ng4 16. Qg5 Rxf3 $1 {. On other moves Black frees the
bishop by ...e5, when his free position is well worth his isolated queen's
pawn.}) 15... Bd7 16. a3 ({If at once} 16. Ne5 Qb4) 16... Rae8 17. Ne5 Re7 18.
Qg3 $2 ({White has played well so far; with} 18. Qe3 {followed by f4, he would
keep black square control and a positional plus. Instead he is tempted by the
tactical threat of Ng6.}) 18... Nh5 $1 {A good sacrifice - though Black didn't
see the consequences and afterwards admitted that the move was a tactical
oversight!} 19. Bxh7+ $2 ({Hindsight suggests} 19. Qh4 {- but most masters
would copy White.}) 19... Kxh7 20. Qg6+ Kg8 21. Nxc6 Bxc6 22. Qxh5 e5 $1 {
Taking the initiative.} 23. Ng3 (23. dxe5 Rxe5 24. Qg4 Bb5 25. Rfe1 Rfe8 {
wins material.}) ({while if} 23. f4 exd4 24. Nxd4 Qc5 25. Rad1 Re4) 23... Qf6
24. a4 ({If} 24. dxe5 Rxe5 25. Qd1 Bb5 {- so White shuts out the bishop.})
24... exd4 25. b4 a6 26. Qd1 $1 {Best.} Be8 {Planning to support his QP's
advance.} 27. Qb3 $2 ({The decisive mistake - a pity in view of White's good
defence.} 27. Ne2 $1 Bh5 28. f3 Qb6 29. Kh1 Qxb4 30. Nxd4 {ought to draw.})
27... Qd6 28. Rad1 Rf4 29. f3 Bg6 {Now White can do little against the QP and
the strong bishop. Black finishes in powerful style.} 30. b5 Re3 31. Qa2 d3 32.
bxa6 bxa6 33. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 34. Rxe1 Qc5+ 35. Kf1 Rc4 36. Qf2 Qxf2+ 37. Kxf2 Rxa4
38. Re5 Rd4 {"I was proud to have won a pawn against so good a player. He won
the British Championship soon after. But when I look at the game now, it looks
pretty bad, especially considering the slow time limit!" (Chris Wood, email
2005). Sources: CB Wood; The Guardian, 2 Apr 1964, p5} (38... Rd4 {If} 39. Ke3
d2 {and otherwise the passed pawn wins further material for Black.}) 0-1

